Carmona patch 2 for llm-d-inference-sim v0.11.4 (commit db3e12bdf8ccf500d93309dc73015fb811296404), applied by docker/sim/build.sh
after disconnect.patch: it is made against the tree that patch leaves and does not apply to the pristine source.
Adds vLLM's per-request timing report (OB-18), so the stack can show the engine's own queue, prefill and generation of a request.

What it does:
 1. A new flag, --enable-per-request-metrics (yaml: enable-per-request-metrics), default off, as the real engine's.
 2. With it, the object "metrics" is written beside "usage" on a whole chat answer and on the final usage chunk of a stream (the one
    with choices: [], which exists when stream_options.include_usage is set): queue_time_ms, time_to_first_token_ms (the prefill: vLLM
    counts it from scheduling), generation_time_ms, mean_itl_ms (only with more than one output token) and tokens_per_second.
    JSON numbers; milliseconds on the simulator's own clock, tokens_per_second per second. Never on a running chunk, on a stream that is
    cut before its usage chunk, or when n > 1 (vLLM sends none then either).
 3. The times are measured, not configured. The queue is from the request reaching the simulator to a worker starting it (close to
    nothing for a request that finds a free worker; the simulator's own queue histogram only sees the ones that waited), the prefill is
    the first-token delay as it was slept, the generation is from the first token to the last one. They ride on the response context
    (Times()). The generation time is set before each token is sent: the writer of a stream builds the final chunk as soon as it has
    the last token. tokens_per_second is the completion tokens over prefill + generation, as vLLM counts from scheduling.
 Not done: the report for /v1/completions, /v1/responses and /v1/messages (chat completions only, the one endpoint inference-service uses).
 Tests: pkg/communication/report_test.go, specs through a real simulator behind a real HTTP server, as disconnect_test.go does. Each guard
 this patch has (the flag, n == 1, one output token, no time to count, the report on the final chunk only) is covered by a spec that
 fails when only that one is removed.

